-- ContinuousBook.daml
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CONTINUOUS SESSION — a price-time-priority limit order book on Canton.
-- ===========================================================================
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `docs/HOW_IT_WORKS.md` lists it as a known limitation, in
-- these words: "A real closing cross inherits the whole resting day book — that is
-- the limit ladder MOC flow walks into. There is no continuous session here, so the
-- ladder must come from LOC orders submitted directly into the auction."
--
-- That is the honest description of a structural hole, and it is worth spelling out
-- WHY it is structural rather than cosmetic. A closing auction does not manufacture
-- a price out of nothing. It inherits one. All day, resting limit orders accumulate
-- on the continuous book — a bid at 99.98, an offer at 100.02, size stacked behind
-- both — and every one of them is a standing, funded, cancellable statement about
-- value. At the close, unpriced market-on-close flow (index trackers, rebalancers,
-- ETF creations: buyers of THE PRINT, with no view on price) walks INTO that ladder.
-- The ladder is what turns "I will buy 400,000 shares at whatever it costs" into a
-- number. Take the ladder away and unpriced flow has nothing to price against: the
-- MOC-vs-MOC book has no price information in it at all, which is exactly why Xetra
-- §11.1.1 step 5 and Euronext Trading Manual §3.1 both fall back to the venue's
-- reference price — an answer, but not a DISCOVERED one.
--
-- A venue with only an auction is therefore a venue whose price comes entirely from
-- limit-on-close orders lodged directly into the cross. That is a real order type and
-- real venues have it, but it is the thin end of the book. This module builds the
-- other end: a continuous session where limit interest RESTS, is matched by price
-- then time, and settles bilaterally at the maker's price the instant it is crossed.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THIS IS, IN ONE PARAGRAPH
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `ContinuousBook` is one instrument's book. Traders `PlaceOrder` — a limit order
-- that rests, or an unpriced market order that must fill immediately — and the
-- backing is reserved at that moment, exactly as `MarketOnClose.reserveExact` does
-- for a sealed order. Each order is stamped with a monotonic `seqNo` from the book:
-- that stamp IS time priority. The venue then runs `MatchOrder`, naming an aggressor
-- and the contra orders it crosses. The choice body checks price-time priority,
-- self-match prevention, the price band and arrival order, walks the ladder, and
-- settles every fill in ONE atomic transaction with the venue as momentary
-- counterparty. Each fill prints to a public `TapePrint` (price, size, time — no
-- identities) and produces a `TradeConfirm` to each side naming the venue as its CCP.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE PRIVACY PROPERTY IS THE SAME ONE THE AUCTION HAS, AND IT IS PRE-TRADE ONLY
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A `RestingOrder` is signed by operator + trader and observed by NOBODY else. While
-- it rests, no other participant — and not even the auditor — can see that it exists,
-- let alone its side, size or limit. That is the dark-pool property, and on a public
-- EVM chain it is unobtainable: the mempool IS the book, so every resting order is a
-- free option written to the fastest reader.
--
-- BUT A DARK POOL IS DARK PRE-TRADE, NOT POST-TRADE, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE
-- WHOLE OF MiFID II. The pre-trade transparency waivers (MiFIR Art. 4 — reference
-- price, negotiated trade, large-in-scale, order-management-facility) are waivers
-- from PRE-trade publication only. Post-trade publication — MiFIR Arts. 6 and 10 for
-- trading venues, Arts. 20 and 21 for investment firms, with the field list in RTS 1 —
-- is mandatory for every venue including dark ones, "as close to real time as is
-- technically possible", and it carries price, volume and time — NOT counterparty
-- identity. `TapePrint` is exactly that record, and it is not decoration:
--
--   IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES THIS VENUE'S PRIORITY CLAIMS AUDITABLE. The
--   assertions below prove the venue matched correctly ACROSS THE ORDERS IT SUPPLIED.
--   They cannot prove it did not WITHHOLD a better-priced resting order, because that
--   order is private to the venue and its owner, and a validator re-executing this
--   transaction never sees it. The tape closes that loop from the other side: a
--   trader whose bid at 100.02 sat unfilled while the tape printed a trade at 100.05
--   is holding ledger-signed evidence of a priority violation. Pre-trade dark,
--   post-trade lit, and the lit half is what polices the dark half.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 🔴 CONTENTION — THE HARD PART, STATED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A continuous book has a problem the auction does not have, it is not a detail, and
-- no amount of Daml makes it go away.
--
-- EVERY CHOICE ON `ContinuousBook` IS CONSUMING. `PlaceOrder` must be, because it
-- stamps `seqNo` and a sequence number that is not allocated exactly once is not a
-- time priority. `MatchOrder` must be, because it moves `liveCount`. `CancelOrder`
-- and `KillOrder` must be, for the same reason. So the book contract is a LOCK on the
-- instrument: Canton's conflict detection serialises any two transactions that touch
-- the same contract, and every order and every match touches this one. Two traders
-- placing orders in the same instant means one of them loses the race, re-reads the
-- book cid and retries.
--
-- THE THROUGHPUT ASSUMPTION THIS DESIGN MAKES, SAID AS A NUMBER: one book commit per
-- Canton confirmation round. Order arrivals and matches on ONE instrument are capped
-- at roughly the domain's sequencing/confirmation rate — order tens per second on a
-- healthy deployment, not the hundreds of thousands per second a colocated matching
-- engine does on one symbol. Anyone claiming otherwise has not read this paragraph.
--
-- WHY THE AUCTION ESCAPES IT, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A CHEAP SHOT AT THIS MODULE. A call
-- auction is a BATCH. It collects interest over minutes and produces exactly ONE
-- crossing transaction. `ClosingAuction.SubmitOrder` is consuming too and has exactly
-- the same contention on submission — the difference is that a batch auction only has
-- to survive it on the collection path, once per session, whereas a continuous book
-- eats it on the HOT path, on every single match. The contention is not new. Its
-- position on the critical path is.
--
-- WHAT WOULD HAVE TO CHANGE TO SCALE IT — three answers, in increasing difficulty.
--
--   1. SHARD BY INSTRUMENT. Already true here and it costs nothing: one
--      `ContinuousBook` per (instrument, cash leg). Two instruments never touch the
--      same contract, so they never conflict and they scale linearly with the number
--      of instruments. This is not a workaround; it is what real exchanges do. Nasdaq
--      and every other major venue distribute symbols across matching engines but keep
--      each symbol on exactly ONE engine, for precisely the reason this module is
--      serialised: price-time priority is a TOTAL ORDER over one book, and you cannot
--      shard a total order without weakening the guarantee it exists to provide. The
--      ledger's constraint here is the same constraint physical venues already live
--      with. What the ledger costs is the constant factor, not the shape.
--
--   2. SPLIT THE SEQUENCER FROM THE BOOK. `PlaceOrder` conflicts with `MatchOrder`
--      today only because both mutate one contract. Move `nextSeq` onto a per-trader
--      `ArrivalStamp` contract (each trader's own stamp, conflicting only with itself)
--      and derive the global order from (stamp value, ledger time). Placement then
--      stops conflicting with matching, and the lock shrinks to the matching path
--      alone. Costs: a heavier order-entry protocol, and a global order that is now
--      DERIVED rather than stamped, so it must be re-validated inside `MatchOrder`.
--      This is the same "shard the counter per participant and sum the shards"
--      escape hatch the auction's `SubmitOrder` header already names.
--
--   3. BATCH THE MATCHES. Nothing forces one match per transaction. `MatchOrder`
--      generalises to a LIST of (aggressor, contras) applied in arrival order inside
--      one commit, which amortises the single book write over N crossings — the
--      throughput of a batch with the semantics of a continuous book, at the cost of
--      latency equal to the batch window. Note where that lands you: a continuous book
--      matched in short batches IS a frequent batch auction (Budish, Cramton & Shim,
--      QJE 2015), which is a defensible market design in its own right and arguably
--      the RIGHT one for a ledger. This module deliberately implements the harder,
--      more conventional thing first so the trade-off can be measured rather than
--      assumed.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 🔴 WHAT THIS IS NOT: IT IS NOT CONNECTED TO THE AUCTION
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Say it plainly. This module compiles standalone against `Holding` and the standard
-- library. It does not import `MarketOnClose`, and `MarketOnClose` does not import it.
-- Closing a continuous session here does NOT hand the resting book to a
-- `ClosingAuction`, so the limitation quoted at the top of this file is NARROWED, not
-- yet closed: the venue now HAS a resting day book; the closing cross still does not
-- inherit it.
--
-- WHAT CONNECTING THEM WOULD TAKE — the honest scope, not a hand-wave:
--
--   a. A HANDOVER CHOICE. `ContinuousBook.HandOverToAuction`, controller operator,
--      taking a `ContractId ClosingAuction` and the live `RestingOrder` cids. For each
--      one it archives the resting order and lodges the equivalent `SealedOrder`
--      through `ClosingAuction.SubmitOrder` — so the auction's `submittedCount` moves
--      with it and the close stays provably complete.
--
--   b. THE BACKING HAS TO MIGRATE, NOT BE RE-RESERVED. A resting order's slice is
--      already carved out and disclosed to the venue. `SubmitOrder` calls
--      `reserveExact` on a holding the TRADER owns, which the reserved slice still is,
--      so the slice can be handed straight in — but the AMOUNTS differ and that is the
--      real work. A resting bid reserves `quantity * limit`; the auction wants the
--      same for an LOC, so a limit order ports one-for-one. An unpriced order does not
--      port at all: it is IOC here by construction, so there is nothing unpriced left
--      resting at the close. The genuinely awkward case is a resting bid whose limit
--      sits outside the AUCTION's collar — it is fundable here and not there.
--
--   c. A DIRECTION FOR THE DEPENDENCY. `MarketOnClose` must not import this module
--      and this module must not import `MarketOnClose`; a handover that names both is
--      a third module (`SessionSchedule`) that imports each, exactly as
--      `MarketOnClose -> LiquidityMandate` runs one way today. Anything else makes the
--      auction unreadable on its own.
--
--   d. THE PRICE LINK, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL PRIZE. Once the ladder is inherited, the
--      auction's `referencePrice` should stop being a venue-set anchor and become the
--      LAST CONTINUOUS PRINT — which is what `TapePrint` already records, and what
--      NYSE Rule 7.35B calls the Continuous Book Clearing Price. That is the single
--      highest-value follow-up in this repo: it is the step at which the closing price
--      stops being anchored on a number the venue supplied and starts being anchored
--      on trades that actually happened.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DELIBERATE DUPLICATION, DECLARED
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `reserveBacking` here is `MarketOnClose.reserveExact` re-written, and `TradeConfirm`
-- overlaps `Settlement.SettlementReceipt`. That is not an oversight. This module is
-- required to compile standalone against `Holding` + stdlib so the auction and the
-- continuous session can be reviewed, and broken, independently. The merge — one
-- shared `reserveBacking` in `Holding`, one shared receipt in `Settlement` — belongs
-- in the same change as (c) above, where the dependency direction gets decided once.
-- ===========================================================================

module ContinuousBook where

import Holding
import DA.List (dedup)
import DA.Optional (isNone, optionalToList)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- BookSide — which side of the market an order is on.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deliberately NOT `Buy`/`Sell`. A continuous book is described from the book's point
-- of view, not the order's: interest to buy rests on the BID and interest to sell
-- rests on the ASK (the offer). An aggressor carries a side too — a taker on the Bid
-- is buying, and it takes from the Ask side. `opposite` is the only mapping needed.
-- (The auction's `Side` type is a separate declaration in a separate module and the
-- two never meet, which is exactly the isolation this module was asked for.)
data BookSide = Bid | Ask
  deriving (Eq, Show)

opposite : BookSide -> BookSide
opposite Bid = Ask
opposite Ask = Bid

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TimeInForce — how long the order lives if it is not filled.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--   GTC — good till cancelled: the unfilled remainder RESTS and keeps its queue
--         position (see `RestingOrder.Fill`). The ordinary limit order.
--   IOC — immediate or cancel: fill what you can right now, cancel the rest. The only
--         legal instruction for an unpriced order, because an unpriced order that
--         rested would be a free option on the whole future of the market.
--
-- 🔴 AN HONEST LIMIT ON "IMMEDIATE". On a real venue, arrival and matching happen
-- inside one pass of the matching engine, so an IOC never rests for any observable
-- time. Here every order round-trips through the ledger: `PlaceOrder` is one
-- transaction and `MatchOrder` is the next, so an IOC is resting — and cancellable,
-- and (to the venue) visible — for one commit in between. There is no way to fuse
-- them, because the taker cannot name its own contras: it cannot see them. So "IOC"
-- here means "the venue must fill or `KillOrder` it in the next transaction", and the
-- window is one ledger round trip, not zero. That is a property of order lifecycle on
-- a ledger, not of this module.
data TimeInForce = GTC | IOC
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- crossesAt — THE ONE DEFINITION of "would this order trade at this price".
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Used by `MatchOrder`'s eligibility gate and again inside `RestingOrder.Fill`, so
-- the match and the settlement leg can never drift into disagreeing about whether an
-- order crosses. Same shape as `MarketOnClose.eligibleAt`, same reason.
--   * A limited BID trades iff its limit is >= the price (it will pay at least P).
--   * A limited ASK trades iff its limit is <= the price (it will accept P).
--   * An UNPRICED order trades at any price. That is the definition of a market order.
crossesAt : BookSide -> Decimal -> Optional Decimal -> Bool
crossesAt _   _ None       = True
crossesAt Bid p (Some lim) = lim >= p
crossesAt Ask p (Some lim) = lim <= p

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The session price band — the collar, borrowed wholesale from the auction.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `bandFraction` of the session anchor, either side. Two jobs, and the second is the
-- one that makes market orders possible at all:
--   1. NO FILL PRINTS OUTSIDE IT. A market order that sweeps a book with a stale
--      far-away offer in it is the classic disaster — stub quotes in the 6 May 2010
--      flash crash, Knight Capital in 2012. A band is what every real venue answers it
--      with (US LULD, Xetra volatility interruptions, Euronext collars).
--   2. IT BOUNDS WHAT AN UNPRICED BID CAN OWE. A limited bid can be funded because its
--      own limit is the bound on what it may pay. An unpriced bid has no limit, so it
--      has no natural bound — and you cannot reserve infinity. The band supplies one:
--      the highest price this book can legally print is `bandHigh`, exactly, so a
--      market bid reserving `quantity * bandHigh` is sufficient BY CONSTRUCTION, not
--      by estimate. Unspent cash comes back as change in the settlement transaction.
--      This is `MarketOnClose`'s collar argument, applied to the same problem.
--
-- 🔴 STATIC BY DESIGN, AND HERE IS THE COST. `referencePrice` is never rewritten by
-- any choice on this template, which is what makes the reservation above sound: the
-- number the reservation was sized against at placement is the number the band is
-- still centred on at the match. A real venue's band moves with the last trade. A
-- moving band on a PRE-FUNDED order would silently invalidate every reservation taken
-- against the old one, so moving it needs a re-reservation protocol per resting order,
-- which is a much larger change than one mutable field.
bandLowOf : Decimal -> Decimal -> Decimal
bandLowOf ref frac = ref * (1.0 - frac)

bandHighOf : Decimal -> Decimal -> Decimal
bandHighOf ref frac = ref * (1.0 + frac)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- priorityKey / aheadOf / inPriorityOrder — PRICE PRIORITY, THEN TIME PRIORITY.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The rule every lit venue in the world runs on, and the reason a resting order is
-- worth placing at all: better price wins; at the same price, earlier arrival wins.
-- If a venue could serve a later order at the same price first, posting liquidity
-- would be a mug's game and nobody would post any.
--
--   * On the ASK side (offers) the BEST price is the LOWEST — a buyer wants the
--     cheapest offer — so the key ascends with price.
--   * On the BID side the BEST price is the HIGHEST, so the key ascends with the
--     NEGATED price. One function, two sides, no duplicated comparison logic.
--   * Ties break on `seqNo`, which the book stamps monotonically at placement. Lower
--     seqNo arrived first and is served first.
--
-- These are PURE functions over the order record on purpose: `MatchOrder` asserts the
-- venue's supplied contra list satisfies them, so the priority rule is a checkable
-- fact in the transaction rather than a promise in a document.
priorityKey : BookSide -> RestingOrder -> (Decimal, Int)
priorityKey side o =
  -- A resting order is always priced — `PlaceOrder` refuses to rest an unpriced one
  -- and `MatchOrder` re-checks it before this is ever called. The 0.0 is unreachable.
  let p = case o.limitPrice of Some l -> l; None -> 0.0
  in case side of
       Ask -> (p, o.seqNo)
       Bid -> (negate p, o.seqNo)

-- Strict, because two distinct orders can never share a seqNo: the book allocates it
-- once and never reuses it. A non-strict comparison would let a duplicated order pass.
aheadOf : BookSide -> RestingOrder -> RestingOrder -> Bool
aheadOf side a b =
  let (pa, sa) = priorityKey side a
      (pb, sb) = priorityKey side b
  in pa < pb || (pa == pb && sa < sb)

inPriorityOrder : BookSide -> [RestingOrder] -> Bool
inPriorityOrder _    []              = True
inPriorityOrder _    [_]             = True
inPriorityOrder side (a :: b :: rest) = aheadOf side a b && inPriorityOrder side (b :: rest)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Execution — one bilateral fill. What `walkBook` produces and the paperwork consumes.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NOTE WHAT IS ABSENT: a continuous book has no pro-rata allocation and no pooled
-- rounding residual. Every fill is ONE buyer against ONE seller at ONE price, so
-- `cashAmount` is computed exactly once and used both for the delivery and for the
-- proceeds. The auction needs `settleResidual` and `distribute` precisely because it
-- rations a marginal price level across many orders and the products do not sum in
-- fixed-precision Decimal. Here there is nothing to ration, so there is nothing to
-- reconcile — the ledger movement and the confirm are literally the same number.
data Execution = Execution
  with
    price      : Decimal  -- the MAKER's limit: the resting order's posted price
    quantity   : Decimal  -- units of the asset that changed hands
    cashAmount : Decimal  -- quantity * price, computed once
    buyer      : Party    -- received the asset
    seller     : Party    -- received the cash
    aggressor  : Party    -- the taker on this fill (fee-tier: "Taker")
    maker      : Party    -- the resting side (fee-tier: "Maker")
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- MatchResult — everything `MatchOrder` hands back. The book cid must be threaded
-- forward by the caller (every choice on the book is consuming — see CONTENTION).
data MatchResult = MatchResult
  with
    book              : ContractId ContinuousBook          -- the successor book
    executions        : [Execution]                        -- the fills, in walk order
    prints            : [ContractId TapePrint]             -- the public tape
    residualAggressor : Optional (ContractId RestingOrder) -- unfilled remainder, if it rests
    residualContra    : [ContractId RestingOrder]          -- partially filled maker, if any
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- ===========================================================================
-- ContinuousBook — one instrument's continuous session.
-- ===========================================================================
-- Signed by the operator; observed by the auditor and the registered participants, so
-- they can see that a session exists and place into it. They CANNOT see each other's
-- orders: those live on `RestingOrder`, whose observer line is empty.
template ContinuousBook
  with
    operator       : Party    -- the venue
    auditor        : Party    -- compliance; sees the session and the tape, not the book
    participants   : [Party]  -- who may place orders here
    instrumentId   : Text     -- the asset being traded ("DEMO:AAPL" | "cETH" | ...)
    cashInstrument : Text     -- the cash leg ("USDC")
    -- THE SESSION ANCHOR. Never rewritten by any choice — see `bandLowOf`/`bandHighOf`
    -- for why that immutability is load-bearing for market-order funding.
    referencePrice : Decimal
    bandFraction   : Decimal  -- 0.10 = ±10%. No fill prints outside the band.
    -- TIME PRIORITY, AS A LEDGER FACT. Stamped onto every order at placement and never
    -- reused. `MatchOrder` reads it to prove the aggressor really is the latest arrival
    -- and that the contra list is in queue order. This is the continuous-book
    -- equivalent of `ClosingAuction.submittedCount` — a monotonic counter the venue
    -- cannot rewind, which is what turns "we matched fairly" into something a
    -- validator re-executes.
    nextSeq        : Int
    -- HOW MANY ORDERS ARE RESTING RIGHT NOW. Moved by placement (+1), by cancellation
    -- (−1) and by every order a match consumes in full. Combined with `ensure
    -- liveCount >= 0` it is a tripwire: an accounting bug that loses an order takes the
    -- next transaction down instead of quietly under-stating the book.
    liveCount      : Int
    isOpen         : Bool     -- False = session closed: no new orders, no matching.
                              -- Cancellation stays open, as it does in a real halt.
  where
    signatory operator
    observer auditor :: participants

    ensure referencePrice > 0.0
        && bandFraction > 0.0 && bandFraction < 1.0
        && nextSeq >= 0
        && liveCount >= 0

    -- =====================================================================
    -- PLACE ORDER — a participant lodges an order and pre-commits its backing.
    -- =====================================================================
    -- CONSUMING, and the reason is `nextSeq`. A time priority is only a priority if
    -- every order gets a distinct stamp and the stamps reflect arrival order; a
    -- counter that two concurrent transactions can both read as 7 is not a queue. So
    -- placement serialises through this contract. That is the contention cost, paid
    -- knowingly and documented at the top of this file. Returns the NEW book cid
    -- alongside the order — every caller must thread it forward.
    choice PlaceOrder : (ContractId ContinuousBook, ContractId RestingOrder)
      with
        trader      : Party
        side        : BookSide
        quantity    : Decimal
        -- `Some p` — a LIMIT order: it rests at p and never trades worse than p.
        -- `None`   — a MARKET order: takes whatever the book shows, must be IOC.
        limitPrice  : Optional Decimal
        timeInForce : TimeInForce
        holdingCid  : ContractId Holding  -- cash (Bid) or the asset (Ask)
      controller trader
      do
        assertMsg "the continuous session is closed"  isOpen
        assertMsg "quantity must be positive"         (quantity > 0.0)
        assertMsg "submitter must be a registered participant" (trader `elem` participants)
        assertMsg "a stated limit must be positive (omit it entirely for a market order)"
          (case limitPrice of None -> True; Some p -> p > 0.0)
        -- AN UNPRICED ORDER MAY NOT REST. A resting market order is a standing promise
        -- to trade at any price at any future time — a free option handed to whoever
        -- next crosses it, and the reason no venue lets one sit on the book. It may
        -- TAKE (that is what a market order is for) and its remainder is cancelled.
        assertMsg "an unpriced (market) order must be immediate-or-cancel — it cannot rest"
          (case limitPrice of None -> timeInForce == IOC; Some _ -> True)
        let lo = bandLowOf  referencePrice bandFraction
            hi = bandHighOf referencePrice bandFraction
        -- A limit outside the band could never legally print here, so accepting it
        -- would be accepting an order the venue knows can never trade — and, worse,
        -- locking up the trader's backing behind it. Reject at the door.
        assertMsg "a limit outside the session price band could never trade here"
          (case limitPrice of None -> True; Some p -> p >= lo && p <= hi)
        h <- fetch holdingCid
        assertMsg "committed holding must belong to the trader" (h.owner == trader)
        -- ...AND IT MUST BE THE RIGHT TOKEN. An ask delivers the ASSET, a bid delivers
        -- the CASH leg; those are the only two instruments an order here can commit.
        -- The auction learned this the expensive way: a mis-instrumented order that
        -- rests is a denial of service on everyone, because it detonates deep inside
        -- settlement where the failure names neither the order nor the book.
        let expectedInstrument = case side of
              Ask -> instrumentId
              Bid -> cashInstrument
        assertMsg "committed holding is the wrong instrument for this side of the book"
          (h.instrumentId == expectedInstrument)
        -- RESERVE THE EXACT BACKING. Carve off exactly what this order can ever owe
        -- into a dedicated slice only this order references, disclosed to the venue so
        -- the match can move it. Change returns to the trader immediately and stays
        -- private. Because the slice belongs to this order alone, no later order,
        -- split, merge or re-placement can consume it and make the pinned cid go
        -- stale — the match always finds valid backing.
        --   ASK          — `quantity` of the asset. Price-independent: an ask delivers
        --                  the units it offered whatever it prints at.
        --   LIMITED BID  — `quantity * limit`. A bid can only ever execute at or inside
        --                  its own limit, so its limit IS the exact worst case.
        --   MARKET BID   — `quantity * bandHigh`. No limit, so the BAND is the bound;
        --                  sufficient by construction, not by estimate.
        let reserveAmount = case (side, limitPrice) of
              (Ask, _)      -> quantity
              (Bid, Some l) -> quantity * l
              (Bid, None)   -> quantity * hi
        reserved <- reserveBacking holdingCid reserveAmount operator
        orderCid <- create RestingOrder with
          operator; auditor; trader
          instrumentId; cashInstrument
          side; quantity; limitPrice; timeInForce
          seqNo = nextSeq
          holdingCid = reserved
        bookCid <- create this with
          nextSeq   = nextSeq + 1
          liveCount = liveCount + 1
        pure (bookCid, orderCid)

    -- =====================================================================
    -- MATCH ORDER — the matching engine. One aggressor, the contra ladder it crosses,
    -- every fill settled in ONE atomic transaction.
    -- =====================================================================
    -- WHY THE VENUE SUPPLIES THE CONTRA LIST AND THE TAKER DOES NOT. The taker cannot
    -- name its counterparties because it cannot SEE them — a `RestingOrder` is private
    -- to the venue and its owner, which is the entire dark-pool property. So the shape
    -- is the one every real market has: the taker sends an order, the VENUE's engine
    -- matches it. The taker's authority to move its own money is nonetheless present
    -- at the moment it moves, because the money moves inside a choice on a contract the
    -- taker SIGNED (`RestingOrder.Fill`). Same authority trick the auction's close uses,
    -- and the reason multi-party settlement composes into one transaction here at all.
    --
    -- WHAT THIS CHOICE PROVES, AND WHAT IT CANNOT — READ BOTH.
    --   PROVEN, by assertion, re-executed by every validator:
    --     * the aggressor is the LATEST arrival (every contra's seqNo is lower), so a
    --       resting order can never be turned into a taker after the fact;
    --     * the contra list is in strict price-then-time priority;
    --     * every contra genuinely crosses the aggressor's limit;
    --     * every contra prices inside the session band;
    --     * no self-matching;
    --     * no duplicates, and the list does not run past the end of the fill, so the
    --       venue cannot pad it with orders that fill nothing;
    --     * every contra ahead of the last one fills IN FULL — the venue cannot take a
    --       nibble out of a better-priced maker and move on to a worse one. This one is
    --       structural rather than merely asserted: `walkBook` fills
    --       `min(aggressor, contra)` and the operator supplies no quantity at all, so
    --       only the LAST order touched can end up partially filled. The assertion in
    --       `walkBook` is the tripwire that keeps it true if that ever changes;
    --     * the trade price is the MAKER's posted limit, COMPUTED here, never supplied.
    --       The operator has no price discretion anywhere in this choice.
    --   NOT PROVEN: that the venue did not WITHHOLD a better-priced resting order. That
    --   order is private to the venue and its owner and a validator re-executing this
    --   transaction never sees it, so no assertion in this body can reach it. The
    --   answer is post-trade, not pre-trade: see `TapePrint`. Do not claim otherwise.
    choice MatchOrder : MatchResult
      with
        aggressorCid : ContractId RestingOrder
        -- The contra ladder, BEST FIRST. Order is not a hint — it is asserted.
        contraCids   : [ContractId RestingOrder]
      controller operator
      do
        assertMsg "the continuous session is closed" isOpen
        assertMsg "a match needs at least one contra order" (not (null contraCids))
        assertMsg "the same resting order cannot appear twice in one match"
          (length (dedup contraCids) == length contraCids)
        assertMsg "the aggressor cannot match against itself"
          (aggressorCid `notElem` contraCids)

        aggr    <- fetch aggressorCid
        contras <- forA contraCids fetch

        -- SAME BOOK. An order from another venue, instrument or cash leg is not
        -- matchable here — and pooling the wrong token is how a settlement blows up
        -- with a message that names nothing.
        let sameBook o = o.operator       == operator
                      && o.instrumentId   == instrumentId
                      && o.cashInstrument == cashInstrument
        assertMsg "the aggressor is not an order in this book"   (sameBook aggr)
        assertMsg "a contra order is not an order in this book"  (all sameBook contras)
        assertMsg "every contra must be on the opposite side of the book"
          (all (\o -> o.side == opposite aggr.side) contras)

        -- SELF-MATCH PREVENTION. A trader crossing its own resting order prints a
        -- trade with no change of beneficial ownership: wash volume that inflates the
        -- tape, distorts the price, and — since the tape is what audits priority here —
        -- corrupts the very record that polices this venue. Real venues run it as a
        -- named service (Nasdaq STP, CME Self-Match Prevention), and MiFID II RTS 6
        -- requires an investment firm's algorithmic trading systems to prevent its own
        -- orders from executing against each other.
        assertMsg "self-matching is not permitted"
          (all (\o -> o.trader /= aggr.trader) contras)

        -- ARRIVAL ORDER. The aggressor is by definition the order that arrived LAST;
        -- everything it crosses was already resting. Without this a venue could
        -- nominate an OLD order as the aggressor and let a newly arrived one collect
        -- maker priority it never earned — jumping the queue in the one direction the
        -- price checks below would not catch.
        assertMsg "the aggressor must be the most recently arrived order in the match"
          (all (\o -> o.seqNo < aggr.seqNo) contras)

        -- PRICE PRIORITY, THEN TIME PRIORITY, over the whole supplied ladder.
        assertMsg "the contra list is not in price-then-time priority order"
          (inPriorityOrder (opposite aggr.side) contras)

        -- Every contra must be PRICED (nothing unpriced can rest) and must actually
        -- cross the aggressor's limit. A market aggressor (`limitPrice = None`) crosses
        -- everything, which is what makes it a market order.
        assertMsg "a contra order does not cross the aggressor's limit"
          (all (\o -> case o.limitPrice of
                        None   -> False
                        Some p -> crossesAt aggr.side p aggr.limitPrice) contras)

        -- THE BAND. Every trade price here is a maker's limit, so checking the makers
        -- checks every print — and it is what makes the market-bid reservation sound.
        let lo = bandLowOf  referencePrice bandFraction
            hi = bandHighOf referencePrice bandFraction
        assertMsg "a contra order prices outside the session price band"
          (all (\o -> case o.limitPrice of
                        None   -> False
                        Some p -> p >= lo && p <= hi) contras)

        -- WALK THE LADDER AND SETTLE. Every leg of every fill moves inside this one
        -- transaction: all of it commits or none of it does. There is no leg risk, no
        -- partially-settled trade, and no window in which the venue is holding one side.
        (execs, aggrResidual, contraResiduals) <- walkBook aggressorCid contraCids

        -- TIME IN FORCE. A GTC remainder RESTS (keeping its seqNo — see `Fill`). An IOC
        -- remainder is cancelled here, in the same transaction as the fills, with its
        -- backing returned to the trader and un-disclosed.
        killed <- case (aggrResidual, aggr.timeInForce) of
          (Some rc, IOC) -> do
            _ <- exercise rc VenueRelease
            pure True
          _ -> pure False
        let restingAggr = if killed then None else aggrResidual

        now <- getTime

        -- THE PUBLIC TAPE. One print per fill: price, size, time — and NO identities.
        -- Post-trade transparency, which is mandatory even for a dark venue, and which
        -- is the only mechanism by which a trader can ever detect that its better-priced
        -- order was passed over. See the header.
        prints <- forA execs (\e -> create TapePrint with
          operator; auditor; participants
          instrumentId; cashInstrument
          price = e.price; quantity = e.quantity
          printedAt = now; matchSeq = aggr.seqNo)

        -- THE BILATERAL CONFIRMS. Two per fill, each naming the trader and the VENUE —
        -- never the other trader. The venue is the momentary counterparty on both legs,
        -- so neither side learns who it traded with, which is the anonymity a central
        -- counterparty exists to provide. `liquidity` is the maker/taker flag every
        -- real fee schedule is built on, and it lets a maker verify it was paid its own
        -- posted price rather than the taker's.
        _ <- forA execs (\e -> do
          _ <- create TradeConfirm with
                 operator; auditor; trader = e.aggressor
                 instrumentId; cashInstrument
                 side = if e.aggressor == e.buyer then Bid else Ask
                 quantity = e.quantity; price = e.price; cashAmount = e.cashAmount
                 liquidity = "Taker"; tradedAt = now
          create TradeConfirm with
                 operator; auditor; trader = e.maker
                 instrumentId; cashInstrument
                 side = if e.maker == e.buyer then Bid else Ask
                 quantity = e.quantity; price = e.price; cashAmount = e.cashAmount
                 liquidity = "Maker"; tradedAt = now)

        -- BOOK ACCOUNTING. Every order in this match is consumed; the ones that left a
        -- residual are back on the book. `ensure liveCount >= 0` is the tripwire if this
        -- arithmetic is ever wrong.
        let stillResting = length (optionalToList restingAggr) + length contraResiduals
        let consumed     = 1 + length contraCids - stillResting
        bookCid <- create this with liveCount = liveCount - consumed

        pure MatchResult with
          book              = bookCid
          executions        = execs
          prints
          residualAggressor = restingAggr
          residualContra    = contraResiduals

    -- =====================================================================
    -- CANCEL ORDER — the trader pulls its OWN resting order.
    -- =====================================================================
    -- Routed THROUGH the book, for the same reason the auction routes withdrawals
    -- through the auction: `liveCount` lives here, and an order that vanished without
    -- the book knowing would leave the count over-stating the live book forever.
    -- `RestingOrder.Release` is controlled by `trader, operator`; this is the one place
    -- both authorities are in scope — the trader controls this choice, and the
    -- operator's authority arrives because it signs the book being exercised. So the
    -- trader keeps the unilateral right to cancel (the venue cannot cancel for them
    -- through this door) and the count moves in the same transaction or not at all.
    --
    -- ALLOWED WHILE THE SESSION IS CLOSED, deliberately. Locking a trader's collateral
    -- inside an order it cannot pull because the venue halted is not a risk control, it
    -- is a hostage. Real venues accept cancels during halts for exactly this reason.
    choice CancelOrder : (ContractId ContinuousBook, ContractId Holding)
      with
        trader   : Party
        orderCid : ContractId RestingOrder
      controller trader
      do
        o <- fetch orderCid
        assertMsg "only the trader who placed an order can pull it" (o.trader == trader)
        assertMsg "that order does not belong to this book"
          (o.operator == operator && o.instrumentId == instrumentId
             && o.cashInstrument == cashInstrument)
        released <- exercise orderCid Release
        bookCid  <- create this with liveCount = liveCount - 1
        pure (bookCid, released)

    -- =====================================================================
    -- KILL ORDER — the venue clears an order off the book.
    -- =====================================================================
    -- The exchange's cancel: an unmatched IOC, an erroneous order, a symbol halt. The
    -- backing goes straight back to the trader, privately — this cancels the ORDER,
    -- never the balance. The operator can do this because it signs the resting order;
    -- taking that away is not possible and would not help, since the power it confers
    -- is the power to REFUSE service, which no venue design can remove from an operator.
    -- What it emphatically is NOT is a path to the trader's money: `VenueRelease` moves
    -- the slice to the trader and nowhere else.
    choice KillOrder : (ContractId ContinuousBook, ContractId Holding)
      with
        orderCid : ContractId RestingOrder
      controller operator
      do
        o <- fetch orderCid
        assertMsg "that order does not belong to this book"
          (o.operator == operator && o.instrumentId == instrumentId
             && o.cashInstrument == cashInstrument)
        released <- exercise orderCid VenueRelease
        bookCid  <- create this with liveCount = liveCount - 1
        pure (bookCid, released)

    -- =====================================================================
    -- OPEN / CLOSE SESSION — the trading day.
    -- =====================================================================
    -- Closing the continuous session stops new orders and stops matching. GTC orders
    -- KEEP RESTING across it, which is what "good till cancelled" means and how a real
    -- day book carries over.
    --
    -- 🔴 THIS IS WHERE THE AUCTION HANDOVER WOULD GO, AND IT IS NOT HERE. On a real
    -- venue, the end of continuous trading is the moment the resting book is handed to
    -- the closing call. `CloseSession` deliberately does nothing but flip the flag —
    -- see "WHAT THIS IS NOT" in the header for exactly what the handover would take.
    choice CloseSession : ContractId ContinuousBook
      controller operator
      do create this with isOpen = False

    choice OpenSession : ContractId ContinuousBook
      controller operator
      do create this with isOpen = True

-- ===========================================================================
-- RestingOrder — one PRIVATE order in the continuous book.
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE DARK-POOL PRIMITIVE, again. Signed by operator + trader, observed by NOBODY
-- else: not other participants, not the auditor. While it rests, only the venue and
-- the trader who placed it know it exists, and nobody at all knows its side, size or
-- limit. On a transparent chain a resting limit order is a free option written to the
-- fastest reader — this is the property that cannot be bought there at any price.
template RestingOrder
  with
    operator       : Party
    auditor        : Party
    trader         : Party
    instrumentId   : Text
    cashInstrument : Text
    side           : BookSide
    quantity       : Decimal          -- units still working (a partial fill reduces it)
    limitPrice     : Optional Decimal -- Some p = limit; None = market (IOC only)
    timeInForce    : TimeInForce
    -- ARRIVAL STAMP — the book's `nextSeq` at placement. Never changes, INCLUDING
    -- across a partial fill, which is exactly how a real venue treats queue position:
    -- being filled does not send you to the back. (What DOES lose priority is amending
    -- the price or INCREASING the size — a new order, a new stamp. This module has no
    -- amend choice, so the question does not arise; adding one means allocating a fresh
    -- seqNo for a price change or a size increase, and keeping it for a size decrease.)
    seqNo          : Int
    holdingCid     : ContractId Holding  -- the reserved slice, disclosed to the operator
  where
    signatory operator, trader

    ensure quantity > 0.0
        && seqNo >= 0
        && (case limitPrice of None -> True; Some p -> p > 0.0)

    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- FILL — deliver this order's leg of one crossing into the venue.
    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Exercised twice per fill by `walkBook` (once on each side). The trader's
    -- authority — needed to split, transfer and disclose its own holding — is present
    -- because the trader SIGNS this contract; that is the whole reason multi-party
    -- settlement composes into a single transaction on this ledger.
    --
    -- Returns (the leg delivered to the venue, the residual order if any remains).
    --
    -- ⚠️ THE TRUST ASSUMPTION THIS CHOICE CARRIES — the same one
    -- `MarketOnClose.SealedOrder.PledgeToVenue` carries, and it must be stated in the
    -- same words. The choice is `controller operator`. Nothing in this body references
    -- the book, the match, or the other side of the trade. So the operator CAN exercise
    -- it standalone at any `tradePrice` inside the trader's limit and take the delivered
    -- leg while delivering nothing back. Every economic control in this design — the
    -- priority assertions, the band, the maker-price rule, the confirms — lives in
    -- `MatchOrder`, one level ABOVE the choice that actually moves the money. The
    -- correct shape is allocate → approve → settle, and this package already implements
    -- it properly in `TokenStandardDvp.daml`; porting settlement onto that lifecycle is
    -- the most valuable change left in both this module and the auction.
    --
    -- WHAT IS DIFFERENT HERE, AND IT IS A REAL IMPROVEMENT RATHER THAN A COMFORT: an
    -- unauthorised standalone `Fill` produces NO `TapePrint` and NO `TradeConfirm`.
    -- Every legitimate fill produces both, and the tape is observed by every
    -- participant. So a stolen leg is not merely visible to its victim — it is visible
    -- as a movement with no print behind it, against a public record the venue itself
    -- signed. That is detection, not prevention. Do not sell it as prevention.
    choice Fill : (ContractId Holding, Optional (ContractId RestingOrder))
      with
        fillQty    : Decimal  -- units crossing on this fill
        tradePrice : Decimal  -- the MAKER's posted price, computed by `walkBook`
      controller operator
      do
        assertMsg "fill quantity must be positive"        (fillQty > 0.0)
        assertMsg "fill cannot exceed the working size"   (fillQty <= quantity)
        assertMsg "trade price must be positive"          (tradePrice > 0.0)
        -- PRICE ELIGIBILITY, through the same `crossesAt` the match gate uses, so the
        -- two can never disagree about whether this order crosses. A market order
        -- passes trivially: an order that accepted the book sight unseen cannot then be
        -- refused settlement for being off-price.
        assertMsg "this order does not trade at that price"
          (crossesAt side tradePrice limitPrice)

        -- WHAT THIS SIDE OWES. An ask delivers the ASSET; a bid delivers the CASH.
        -- `fillQty * tradePrice` is evaluated exactly once for the whole fill (see
        -- `Execution`), so the amount delivered and the amount confirmed are the same
        -- number by construction, not by reconciliation.
        let deliverAmount = case side of
              Ask -> fillQty
              Bid -> fillQty * tradePrice
        (deliveredCid, changeOpt) <- fillFromReserve holdingCid deliverAmount operator

        let remainingQty = quantity - fillQty
        if remainingQty <= 0.0
          then do
            -- FULLY FILLED. Anything left in the reservation is the trader's and goes
            -- home privately. For an ask that is nothing (it delivered exactly the units
            -- it offered). For a bid it is the PRICE IMPROVEMENT — the difference
            -- between the limit it funded and the better prices it actually paid — which
            -- is real money and is returned in this same transaction, not swept.
            _ <- forA (optionalToList changeOpt)
                   (\c -> exercise c Transfer with newOwner = trader)
            pure (deliveredCid, None)
          else do
            -- PARTIALLY FILLED. The remainder rests, KEEPING ITS seqNo — a partial fill
            -- must not cost queue position, or every maker would be punished for being
            -- hit.
            changeCid <- case changeOpt of
              Some c -> pure c
              -- Unreachable: remainingQty > 0 means the reservation strictly exceeded
              -- what was delivered, so `fillFromReserve` split rather than transferred.
              None   -> abort "a partial fill left no change to re-reserve"
            ch <- fetch changeCid
            -- WHAT THE REMAINDER STILL NEEDS BACKING FOR — the same rule as placement,
            -- applied to the size that is still working.
            let requiredAfter = case (side, limitPrice) of
                  (Ask, _)      -> remainingQty
                  (Bid, Some l) -> remainingQty * l
                  -- A market order is IOC, so this residual exists for the remainder of
                  -- ONE transaction before `MatchOrder` kills it. There is no tighter
                  -- bound to compute than "everything left", and everything left is
                  -- sufficient: the reservation was sized at `quantity * bandHigh` and
                  -- every price paid was at or below bandHigh.
                  (Bid, None)   -> ch.amount
            assertMsg "the residual order would be under-backed" (ch.amount >= requiredAfter)
            keptCid <-
              if ch.amount == requiredAfter
                then pure changeCid       -- already disclosed to the operator
                else do
                  -- RELEASE THE SURPLUS NOW, NOT LATER. For a bid that traded inside its
                  -- own limit, `fillQty * (limit − tradePrice)` is money the order can
                  -- never owe. Leaving it locked behind the residual would be a silent
                  -- margin charge for the crime of getting a good fill.
                  (kept, surplus) <- exercise changeCid Split with splitAmount = requiredAfter
                  keptDisclosed <- exercise kept Disclose with newObserver = operator
                  _ <- exercise surplus Transfer with newOwner = trader
                  pure keptDisclosed
            residual <- create this with
              quantity   = remainingQty
              holdingCid = keptCid
            pure (deliveredCid, Some residual)

    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- RELEASE — the trader pulls this order; the reserved backing goes home.
    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Controlled by `trader, operator`, NOT the trader alone: `liveCount` lives on the
    -- book, so an order that could vanish behind the venue's back would corrupt it. The
    -- two authorities meet in `ContinuousBook.CancelOrder`, which is the entry point —
    -- this choice is the mechanism.
    --
    -- Transferring the reserved slice back re-creates it PRIVATE (`Holding.Transfer`
    -- resets `disclosedTo`), so the balance is freely the trader's again AND the venue
    -- stops observing it. Un-reserving and un-disclosing are the same operation.
    choice Release : ContractId Holding
      controller trader, operator
      do exercise holdingCid Transfer with newOwner = trader

    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- VENUE RELEASE — the venue clears this order; the backing still goes home.
    -- -------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Needed as a separate choice because a `controller trader, operator` choice cannot
    -- be exercised from inside `MatchOrder` (a choice on the BOOK): the trader signs
    -- this order, not the book, so the trader's authority is not in scope there. Inside
    -- THIS choice it is, which is what lets the transfer back to the trader happen.
    -- Used for an unmatched IOC remainder and for `ContinuousBook.KillOrder`.
    choice VenueRelease : ContractId Holding
      controller operator
      do exercise holdingCid Transfer with newOwner = trader

-- ===========================================================================
-- TapePrint — the PUBLIC post-trade record of one fill.
-- ===========================================================================
-- Price, size, time, and the sequence number of the match that produced it. NO
-- identities, on either side — which is both what MiFID II RTS 1 post-trade
-- publication actually requires and what a central counterparty exists to provide.
--
-- Observed by the auditor AND every registered participant. That is deliberate and it
-- is the counterweight to everything else in this file being private: the resting book
-- is dark, so the tape is the only public fact about this venue, and it is therefore
-- the only thing that can hold the venue to its own priority rules. A maker whose bid
-- at 100.02 rested untouched while the tape printed at 100.05 has venue-signed
-- evidence of a violation. Without the tape, that violation is undetectable and every
-- priority assertion in `MatchOrder` is only as good as the list the venue chose to
-- supply.
template TapePrint
  with
    operator       : Party
    auditor        : Party
    participants   : [Party]   -- the whole session sees every print
    instrumentId   : Text
    cashInstrument : Text
    price          : Decimal
    quantity       : Decimal
    printedAt      : Time
    matchSeq       : Int       -- the aggressor's seqNo: ties the prints of one sweep
  where
    signatory operator
    observer auditor :: participants
    ensure price > 0.0 && quantity > 0.0

-- ===========================================================================
-- TradeConfirm — the venue's bilateral confirm to ONE side of one fill.
-- ===========================================================================
-- The exchange confirm. Signed by the venue, observed by the one trader it belongs to
-- plus the auditor — and it never names the other trader, because the venue stood
-- between them as momentary counterparty and neither side is entitled to learn who it
-- traded with. Two of these per fill.
--
-- `liquidity` is the maker/taker flag: "Maker" for the resting side, "Taker" for the
-- aggressor. Every fee schedule in the industry is built on that one field (maker
-- rebates, taker fees), and here it also lets a maker verify on its own confirm that
-- it was paid ITS posted price rather than the taker's — the rule `walkBook` enforces.
template TradeConfirm
  with
    operator       : Party
    auditor        : Party
    trader         : Party
    instrumentId   : Text
    cashInstrument : Text
    side           : BookSide  -- this trader's side of this fill
    quantity       : Decimal
    price          : Decimal
    cashAmount     : Decimal   -- quantity * price, the number that actually moved
    liquidity      : Text      -- "Maker" | "Taker"
    tradedAt       : Time
  where
    signatory operator
    observer trader, auditor
    ensure quantity > 0.0 && price > 0.0
        && (liquidity == "Maker" || liquidity == "Taker")

-- ===========================================================================
-- walkBook — WALK THE LADDER: match the aggressor down the contra list, settling
-- each crossing bilaterally, in the caller's single transaction.
-- ===========================================================================
-- The list arrives already validated by `MatchOrder` (opposite side, crossing, in
-- price-then-time priority, inside the band, no self-match, no duplicates, all older
-- than the aggressor). This function's job is the money, and the two rules it enforces
-- that the pre-checks cannot:
--
--   1. THE TRADE PRICE IS THE MAKER'S. `tradePrice` is read off the resting order's
--      own limit — never supplied by the operator, never taken from the aggressor.
--      This is the universal rule of a continuous market and it is the reason posting
--      liquidity pays: the maker gets the price it posted, and any improvement accrues
--      to the taker who crossed. A taker bidding 101 into an offer at 100 pays 100, and
--      the extra 1 comes straight back to it as change in this same transaction.
--
--   2. NO NIBBLING, AND NO PADDING. The fill is `min(aggressor, contra)` and the
--      operator supplies NO quantity anywhere in this choice, so only the last order
--      touched can ever be partially filled — a venue cannot take a slice out of a
--      better-priced maker and carry on to a worse one, because it has no way to
--      express the idea. That is structural. The two assertions below are the
--      tripwires that keep it structural: "a contra ahead of the last fill must be
--      filled in full" (unreachable today, and it must STAY unreachable) and "the
--      contra list runs past the end of the fill", which is reachable and is what
--      stops the venue padding the ladder with orders that trade nothing.
--
-- SETTLEMENT SHAPE — the venue as MOMENTARY counterparty. Each side delivers its leg
-- into the venue (inside a choice on its OWN order, which is where its authority is),
-- and the venue immediately hands each leg to the other side. The venue owns both legs
-- for zero ledger time: there is no commit in which it is holding anyone's money. And
-- because each fill is one buyer against one seller at one price, there is no pooling
-- and no allocation residual to reconcile — the auction needs `mergeAll`/`distribute`
-- and a published residual rule because it rations a marginal level across many
-- orders; a continuous book never rations anything.
walkBook :
     ContractId RestingOrder
  -> [ContractId RestingOrder]
  -> Update ([Execution], Optional (ContractId RestingOrder), [ContractId RestingOrder])
walkBook aggrCid [] =
  -- Ladder exhausted with the aggressor still working: the remainder is unfilled.
  -- `MatchOrder` decides its fate from the time-in-force.
  pure ([], Some aggrCid, [])
walkBook aggrCid (cCid :: rest) = do
  aggr   <- fetch aggrCid
  contra <- fetch cCid
  tradePrice <- case contra.limitPrice of
    Some p -> pure p
    -- Unreachable: `PlaceOrder` refuses to rest an unpriced order and `MatchOrder`
    -- re-checks every contra is priced. Kept as an abort rather than a default so a
    -- future change that breaks the invariant fails loudly instead of trading at 0.
    None   -> abort "a resting order must be priced — an unpriced order cannot make a market"
  let fillQty = if aggr.quantity <= contra.quantity then aggr.quantity else contra.quantity

  -- BOTH LEGS INTO THE VENUE. Each `Fill` runs inside a choice on that trader's own
  -- order, so that trader's authority is present exactly where its money moves.
  (aggrLeg,   aggrResidual)   <- exercise aggrCid Fill with fillQty; tradePrice
  (contraLeg, contraResidual) <- exercise cCid   Fill with fillQty; tradePrice

  let (buyer, seller) = case aggr.side of
        Bid -> (aggr.trader,   contra.trader)
        Ask -> (contra.trader, aggr.trader)

  -- ...AND STRAIGHT OUT AGAIN. The venue's authority to move what it now owns comes
  -- from signing the `ContinuousBook` this was called from.
  case aggr.side of
    Bid -> do
      _ <- exercise aggrLeg   Transfer with newOwner = seller  -- the taker's cash
      _ <- exercise contraLeg Transfer with newOwner = buyer   -- the maker's asset
      pure ()
    Ask -> do
      _ <- exercise aggrLeg   Transfer with newOwner = buyer   -- the taker's asset
      _ <- exercise contraLeg Transfer with newOwner = seller  -- the maker's cash
      pure ()

  let exec = Execution with
        price      = tradePrice
        quantity   = fillQty
        cashAmount = fillQty * tradePrice
        buyer; seller
        aggressor  = aggr.trader
        maker      = contra.trader

  case aggrResidual of
    None -> do
      -- The aggressor is done. Anything further in the list would fill nothing.
      assertMsg "the contra list runs past the end of the fill" (null rest)
      pure ([exec], None, optionalToList contraResidual)
    Some nextAggr -> do
      -- The aggressor still has size, so this contra MUST have been exhausted.
      assertMsg "a contra ahead of the last fill must be filled in full"
        (isNone contraResidual)
      (execs, finalAggr, cres) <- walkBook nextAggr rest
      pure (exec :: execs, finalAggr, cres)

-- ===========================================================================
-- reserveBacking — carve off EXACTLY `amt` from a holding the trader owns into a
-- dedicated slice, disclosed to the operator, and return it.
-- ===========================================================================
-- This is what makes an order's backing STABLE: the order references only its own
-- slice, so no later order, split, merge or re-placement can consume it and make the
-- pinned cid go stale. The unreserved remainder stays with the trader and stays
-- private. Must be called where the holding OWNER's authority is in scope — `Split`
-- and `Disclose` are owner-controlled — and `PlaceOrder` is controlled by the trader,
-- so it is.
--
-- (This is `MarketOnClose.reserveExact`, deliberately re-stated rather than imported;
-- see DELIBERATE DUPLICATION in the header.)
reserveBacking : ContractId Holding -> Decimal -> Party -> Update (ContractId Holding)
reserveBacking holdingCid amt operator = do
  h <- fetch holdingCid
  assertMsg "committed holding is too small to back this order" (h.amount >= amt)
  assertMsg "an order must reserve a positive amount" (amt > 0.0)
  reserved <-
    if h.amount == amt
      then pure holdingCid                 -- exact: reserve the whole holding
      else do
        (r, _change) <- exercise holdingCid Split with splitAmount = amt
        pure r
  exercise reserved Disclose with newObserver = operator

-- ===========================================================================
-- fillFromReserve — deliver EXACTLY `amt` out of a reserved slice to the venue and
-- hand back whatever is left, still disclosed to the venue.
-- ===========================================================================
-- The difference from `Holding.deliverExact` is the CHANGE, and it matters. A partial
-- fill leaves a remainder that must go on backing the residual order, so the change
-- cannot simply be dropped: this returns it, and re-DISCLOSES it to the operator,
-- because `Holding.Split` deliberately makes both successors private and a residual
-- order whose backing the venue cannot see is a residual order that can never be
-- matched again. `Fill` then decides how much of that change the residual actually
-- needs and sends the surplus home.
fillFromReserve :
     ContractId Holding -> Decimal -> Party
  -> Update (ContractId Holding, Optional (ContractId Holding))
fillFromReserve reservedCid amt operator = do
  assertMsg "a delivery must be positive" (amt > 0.0)
  h <- fetch reservedCid
  assertMsg "the reserved backing is too small for this fill" (h.amount >= amt)
  if h.amount == amt
    then do
      delivered <- exercise reservedCid Transfer with newOwner = operator
      pure (delivered, None)
    else do
      (deliverCid, changeCid) <- exercise reservedCid Split with splitAmount = amt
      delivered <- exercise deliverCid Transfer with newOwner = operator
      change    <- exercise changeCid  Disclose with newObserver = operator
      pure (delivered, Some change)
